  <article class="blog-post">

  <h1>Maine Medical Marijuana Patient Delivery Services in 2026</h1>
  <p class="lede">Maine's medical-use law includes delivery within the definition of medical use. Legal medical delivery is tied to authorized medical-program participants and patient eligibility—not to a separate state cannabis courier license.</p>

  <Callout title="No separate medical courier license" type="warning">
    Maine OCP's listed medical-program registrations include caregivers, dispensaries, caregiver retail stores, testing facilities, and manufacturing facilities. The program does not list a general “medical cannabis courier” license or courier endorsement. A delivery provider should be able to identify the registered caregiver or dispensary responsible for the transfer.
  </Callout>

  <section>
    <h2>How Maine medical delivery authority works</h2>
    <p><a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/22/title22sec2421-A.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">22 M.R.S. §2421-A(31)</a> defines “medical use” to include the acquisition, possession, cultivation, manufacture, use, delivery, transfer, or transportation of cannabis or cannabis paraphernalia related to treating or alleviating a qualifying patient's diagnosis or symptoms.</p>
    <p>That definition operates with the specific authorized-conduct provisions in Title 22:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/22/title22sec2423-A.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§2423-A</a> sets out what qualifying patients and caregivers may do.</li>
      <li><a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/22/title22sec2428.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§2428</a> governs registered dispensaries and transfers to qualifying patients and caregivers acting for patients.</li>
      <li><a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/22/title22sec2423-D.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§2423-D</a> governs visiting qualifying patients.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Delivery does not erase the other requirements governing who may provide cannabis, who may receive it, how much may be transferred, or what evidence a patient must present.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Medical and adult-use delivery are different</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Medical use</th><th>Adult use</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Governed primarily by Title 22, chapter 558-C and 18-691 C.M.R. ch. 2</td><td>Governed by Title 28-B and the adult-use rules, including Chapter 30</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Transaction must be for a qualifying patient's medical use</td><td>Recipient must be an adult-use consumer age 21 or older</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Authorized participants include registered caregivers and registered dispensaries acting within medical-program authority</td><td>Specified adult-use establishment licensees may deliver within §504(9)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No general courier-license category</td><td>No standalone courier-license category; stores may not use a third-party delivery service</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>A business holding only an adult-use license is not thereby a medical dispensary, and a medical caregiver registration is not an adult-use delivery license.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Patient checklist before accepting delivery</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Verify your own eligibility.</strong> Maine patients need a current written certification from an authorized medical provider. Visiting patients must satisfy Maine's visiting-patient rule and present the required home-state credentials and identification.</li>
      <li><strong>Identify the provider.</strong> Ask whether the seller is a registered caregiver or registered dispensary and which medical-program registration supports the transfer.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm what you must present.</strong> Ask the provider what written certification, registry credential, and government-issued identification are required at handoff.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm the order and transfer limit.</strong> Medical transaction limits and visiting-patient limits differ from adult-use limits.</li>
      <li><strong>Use a lawful destination.</strong> Medical deliveries may not be knowingly made to a municipally designated safe zone unless otherwise authorized.</li>
      <li><strong>Do not accept shipment by mail or across state lines.</strong> Maine's medical authorization does not override federal restrictions.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Questions to ask a delivery provider</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card"><strong>Who is the registrant?</strong><p>Get the legal or business name of the caregiver or dispensary responsible for the sale.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><strong>Which program?</strong><p>Confirm whether the transaction is medical or adult use; do not rely on a generic “licensed delivery” label.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><strong>What credentials?</strong><p>Confirm exactly what patient certification, visiting-patient documentation, and photo ID must be shown.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><strong>What records?</strong><p>Ask for a receipt and retain the provider's identifying information and order details.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Caregiver delivery</h2>
    <p>A caregiver may assist qualifying patients within the authority and limits of §2423-A. Caregiver activity is not a general on-demand courier marketplace. The caregiver remains responsible for lawful possession, transfer, patient eligibility, applicable registration, and records.</p>
    <p>OCP publishes a <a href="https://www.maine.gov/dafs/ocp/open-data/medical-use/registrant-search" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Medical Use Applicant and Registrant Search</a> and downloadable caregiver data. The public file is a dated snapshot and may not resolve every DBA, delivery identity, or storefront relationship, so compare the provider's identifying information with the current OCP record and ask the provider to resolve any mismatch.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Registered dispensary delivery</h2>
    <p>Section 2428 authorizes a registered dispensary to transfer cannabis to qualifying patients and to caregivers acting on behalf of qualifying patients. The dispensary does not need a fictional courier endorsement; it must operate within its medical dispensary registration and the statutes and rules governing the patient transaction, transport, security, and records.</p>
    <p>Do not assume that an adult-use store and a registered medical dispensary are interchangeable merely because they share a brand or location. Verify which licensed or registered entity is making the transaction.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Visiting patients</h2>
    <p>Maine permits qualifying patients from approved jurisdictions to participate under <a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/22/title22sec2423-D.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§2423-D</a>. OCP maintains the current approved-jurisdiction list and explains the documents a visiting patient must present. The patient's home jurisdiction may impose additional restrictions.</p>
    <p>Review the <a href="https://www.maine.gov/dafs/ocp/medical-use/visiting-patients" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OCP visiting-patient page</a> before ordering. Do not rely on a fixed number of reciprocity states or an assumed validity period from an old guide.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Red flags</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A provider claims to have an OCP “courier license” but will not identify the caregiver or dispensary responsible for the sale.</li>
      <li>A marketplace treats cannabis delivery like an unrelated gig-driver service.</li>
      <li>The provider says patient credentials and identification are unnecessary.</li>
      <li>The seller cannot explain whether the order is medical or adult use.</li>
      <li>The provider offers to mail cannabis or carry it across a state line.</li>
      <li>Products arrive without labeling, order documentation, or a traceable seller.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

